{UTF-8}
{ Chapter 4: asking a warehouse three questions with a step, a pulse and a ramp.
  Set one of Step Size, Pulse Volume or Ramp Slope above zero (one scenario each). }
Step Size=
	0
	~	cases/week
	~	Scenario "Step": 20. A lasting rise in demand from week 5.
	|
Pulse Volume=
	0
	~	cases
	~	Scenario "Pulse": 80. One extra burst of orders during week 5.
	|
Ramp Slope=
	0
	~	cases/week/week
	~	Scenario "Ramp": 2. Demand grows from week 5 to week 40, then levels off.
	|
Demand=
	100 + STEP(Step Size, 5) + Pulse Volume * PULSE(5, Pulse Width) / Pulse Width + RAMP(Ramp Slope, 5, 40)
	~	cases/week
	~	Customer demand: 100 a week plus the chosen test input. Dividing the pulse volume (cases) by its width (weeks) gives a rate.
	|
Pulse Width=
	1
	~	week
	~	|
Expected Demand=
	SMOOTH(Demand, Demand Smoothing Time)
	~	cases/week
	~	What the planner believes demand is: demand averaged over about 4 weeks.
	|
Demand Smoothing Time=
	4
	~	week
	~	|
Inventory Coverage=
	2
	~	week
	~	Weeks of expected demand the warehouse wants to hold.
	|
Desired Inventory=
	Expected Demand * Inventory Coverage
	~	cases
	~	|
Inventory Adjustment Time=
	3
	~	week
	~	How quickly the planner closes the gap between desired and actual inventory.
	|
Production=
	MAX(0, Expected Demand + (Desired Inventory - Inventory) / Inventory Adjustment Time)
	~	cases/week
	~	Replace expected demand and close part of the inventory gap. MAX keeps it from going negative.
	|
Minimum Ship Time=
	0.5
	~	week
	~	The fastest the warehouse can empty its shelves.
	|
Shipments=
	MIN(Demand, Inventory / Minimum Ship Time)
	~	cases/week
	~	Ship what is ordered, but never more than the shelves allow.
	|
Inventory= INTEG(
	Production - Shipments,
	200)
	~	cases
	~	Starts in balance: 200 cases, two weeks of the starting demand of 100 (written as a number so the diagram needs no initial-value link).
	|
INITIAL TIME = 0 ~ week ~ |
FINAL TIME = 60 ~ week ~ |
TIME STEP = 0.25 ~ week ~ |
SAVEPER = 0.25 ~ week ~ |
